  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Justice obligated $9.7B in contract spending in FY2026 to date. The Department of Justice made 48,645 awards with an average award size of $200K. Obligations are up 13.6% year over year. Contracting activity has varied across recent years: FY21 was $9.1B, FY22 was $9B, FY23 was $10B, FY24 was $9.4B, and FY25 was $8.5B. Other Computer Related Services is the largest NAICS category with $1.3B in obligations, followed by Security Guards and Patrol Services at $772M and General Medical and Surgical Hospitals at $728M. METROPOLITAN SECURITY SERVICES, INC. is the top vendor with $524M in obligations, followed by NAPHCARE LLC at $209M and MCKESSON CORPORATION at $194M. The mix of spending shows a blend of IT, security, medical, and legal services purchases. The FY2026 totals are fiscal year to date and therefore partial, but they reflect a notable shift in spending toward computer services and security-related contracts compared with prior fiscal years.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How large is Justice's contracting program this fiscal year?",
        "body": "The Department of Justice obligated $9.7B in FY2026 to date. The Department of Justice made 48,645 awards with an average award size of $200K. Obligations are up 13.6% year over year, and FY25 obligations were $8.5B while FY23 peaked at $10B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What is Justice buying and who wins the work?",
        "body": "Other Computer Related Services leads procurement with $1.3B in obligations, and Security Guards and Patrol Services follows with $772M. METROPOLITAN SECURITY SERVICES, INC. is the largest vendor with $524M, and NAPHCARE LLC and MCKESSON CORPORATION follow with $209M and $194M respectively."
      }
    ]
